Done. Summary of the research run:

**What JEV is:** Repo and Second Brain searches found no internal definition — the term came from outside. Web research resolves it as **Jev, TypeSafe AI's "System One" decision model** (released 2026-09-15): a text-only interface where you serialize a state plus typed questions (Choice / Score / Noul) and get calibrated probabilities back instead of generated prose, trained with RLCD. Three independent 2026 evaluations (agent-trace security judge, CWE classification, network IDS) confirm the pattern. Non-fitting meanings (V-JEPA, JeVois cameras, Japanese Encephalitis Virus) are listed in the doc.

**Fit for TrustShield:** Strong fit as a supplementary layer for fraud text (scam/phishing/legit classification is exactly JEV's one-shot Choice shape), weaker for URL detection since the static analyzer already covers most of it for free. Cost is low (~$0.0002/judgment per the arXiv trace study; $0.042/MTok launch price) but remote-only — which conflicts with the project's local-only privacy posture, so the pilot should feed it only redacted metadata states.

**Recommendation: PILOT, not adopt.** Reasons: the sealed-holdout pipeline for `trustshield_text_v3` would otherwise be replaced by an uncalibrated 3-week-old external model; privacy boundary in `docs/LLM_PRIVACY_BOUNDARY.md` must be respected; and the Rogue analysis warns the decision model isn't the security boundary — fusion integration must be re-validated under the V4 protocol.

